This is a very common structure.

2. First: JavaWebProjectApplication

Your main class:

package com.webproject.JavaWebProject;


import org.springframework.boot.SpringApplication;
import org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.SpringBootApplication;


@SpringBootApplication
public class JavaWebProjectApplication {


    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(JavaWebProjectApplication.class, args);
    }


}

This is the starting point of your Spring Boot application.

When you click the Run button, Java starts here:

public static void main(String[] args)

Then:

SpringApplication.run(JavaWebProjectApplication.class, args);

starts Spring Boot.

What does @SpringBootApplication do?
@SpringBootApplication

is a very important annotation.

For now, think of it as telling Spring:

"Start the Spring application and find the Spring components in this project."

It enables things such as:

component scanning
auto-configuration
Spring Boot configuration
Component scanning is especially important here.

Your main class is:

com.webproject.JavaWebProject

And your other classes are inside:

com.webproject.JavaWebProject.Controller
com.webproject.JavaWebProject.Model
com.webproject.JavaWebProject.Service

Therefore Spring can discover them.

For example:

com.webproject.JavaWebProject
             ↓
       Controller
       Model
       Service
3. Product

Now let's look at your Model.

package com.webproject.JavaWebProject.Model;


public class Product {


    private int prodid;
    private String prodName;
    private int price;


    public Product(int prodid, String prodName, int price) {
        this.prodid = prodid;
        this.prodName = prodName;
        this.price = price;
    }


    // getters and setters
}

This class represents one product.

For example:

Product
----------------
prodid   = 101
prodName = android
price    = 50000

Another product:

Product
----------------
prodid   = 102
prodName = iphone
price    = 420000

So Product is basically your data model.

4. Your ProductServices

Now we have:

@Service
public class ProductServices {


    List<Product> products =
        Arrays.asList(
            new Product(101, "android", 50000),
            new Product(102, "iphone", 420000)
        );


    public List<Product> getProducts() {
        return products;
    }
}

This is the Service layer.

What does @Service mean?
@Service
public class ProductServices

@Service tells Spring:

"Create and manage an object of this class."

So Spring creates a ProductServices object and keeps it inside the Spring container.

You can think:

Spring Container
       |
       └── ProductServices object

Later, another class can ask Spring for this object.

That's exactly what your ProductController does.

5. Creating the products

This line:

List<Product> products =
    Arrays.asList(
        new Product(101, "android", 50000),
        new Product(102, "iphone", 420000)
    );

creates two Product objects.

Conceptually:

Product object 1
    ↓
id = 101
name = android
price = 50000


Product object 2
    ↓
id = 102
name = iphone
price = 420000

And both are placed inside:

List<Product>

So:

products
   ↓
[ Product(101, android, 50000),
  Product(102, iphone, 420000) ]
6. getProducts()

Then:

public List<Product> getProducts() {
    return products;
}

This simply returns the list.

So if someone asks the service:

productServices.getProducts();

the service returns:

[
    Product(101, "android", 50000),
    Product(102, "iphone", 420000)
]
7. Now the most important part — ProductController

Your controller:

@RestController
public class ProductController {


    @Autowired
    ProductServices productServices;


    @RequestMapping("/product")
    public List<Product> getProducts() {
        return productServices.getProducts();
    }
}

This is responsible for handling the HTTP request.

8. @RestController
@RestController
public class ProductController

This tells Spring:

"This class contains methods that handle HTTP requests and return data to the client."

So Spring registers ProductController as a controller.

9. @RequestMapping("/product")
@RequestMapping("/product")

This means:

If someone requests /product, execute this method.

Suppose your application is running at:

http://localhost:8080

Then the complete URL is:

http://localhost:8080/product

When you open this URL, Spring looks for a controller that handles:

/product

It finds:

@RequestMapping("/product")
public List<Product> getProducts()

and executes it.

10. What is @Autowired doing?

This is an extremely important Spring concept.

You have:

@Autowired
ProductServices productServices;

You are basically saying:

"Spring, give me the ProductServices object that you created."

Remember:

@Service
public class ProductServices

tells Spring to create a ProductServices object.

Then:

@Autowired
ProductServices productServices;

asks Spring to inject that object into the controller.

Conceptually:

Spring Container
       |
       | has
       ↓
ProductServices object
       |
       | inject
       ↓
ProductController
       |
       └── productServices

This is called Dependency Injection.

Your controller depends on ProductServices, so Spring provides that dependency.

11. Then getProducts() executes

When /product is requested:

public List<Product> getProducts() {
    return productServices.getProducts();
}

First:

productServices

is the ProductServices object injected by Spring.

Then:

productServices.getProducts()

calls:

public List<Product> getProducts() {
    return products;
}

So the service returns the list.

12. What does the browser receive?

The controller returns:

List<Product>

But because this is:

@RestController

Spring converts the Java objects into JSON.

So the browser/API client will receive something like:

[
    {
        "prodid": 101,
        "prodName": "android",
        "price": 50000
    },
    {
        "prodid": 102,
        "prodName": "iphone",
        "price": 420000
    }
]

This conversion from Java object to JSON is handled by Spring's HTTP message conversion mechanism, commonly using Jackson in a standard Spring Boot setup.

13. Complete flow

This is the most important thing to understand.

Suppose you open:

http://localhost:8080/product
Step 1 — Browser sends request
GET /product

↓

Step 2 — Spring finds Controller
@RestController
public class ProductController

↓

Step 3 — Spring finds matching URL
@RequestMapping("/product")

↓

Step 4 — Spring executes
getProducts()

↓

Step 5 — Controller asks Service
productServices.getProducts()

↓

Step 6 — Service returns the list
return products;

↓

Step 7 — Controller returns the list
return productServices.getProducts();

↓

Step 8 — Spring converts Java objects to JSON
List<Product>
       ↓
      JSON

↓

Step 9 — Browser receives
[
  {
    "prodid": 101,
    "prodName": "android",
    "price": 50000
  },
  {
    "prodid": 102,
    "prodName": "iphone",
    "price": 420000
